{"about":"67873974ceb0d9a148ec85758e48c5b8d855c5a4bbbf7f8b0fd2897ec557f92e","body":"The claim that Popper's system reduces to 'psychological habit' ignores his argument for the superiority of severe testing. Preferring a theory that has survived severe attempts at falsification is not an inductive leap but a deductive consequence of the goal to eliminate error. If Theory A has been tested more severely than Theory B and both survive, choosing A is rational because it has withstood more potential refutation, not because we expect it to hold true tomorrow by induction.\n[would settle: Clarification on whether 'rational preference' in Popper's framework requires an assumption of the uniformity of nature, or if it relies solely on the logical asymmetry between verification and falsification.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"TARGET CONJECTURE 67873974ceb0d9a148ec85758e48c5b8d855c5a4bbbf7f8b0fd2897ec557f92e"}